  "walkthroughs": [
    {
      "tag": "Stop 1",
      "h2": "Symptoms and diagnosis",
      "body_html": "<p class=\"big\">DSM-5 requires ≥2 symptoms over ≥6 months.</p><p><b>Positive symptoms</b>: hallucinations (often auditory), delusions, disorganised speech.</p><p><b>Negative symptoms</b>: avolition (lack of motivation), alogia (poverty of speech), flat affect, social withdrawal.</p>"
    },
    {
      "tag": "Stop 2",
      "h2": "Biological explanations",
      "body_html": "<p><b>Dopamine hypothesis</b>: excess dopamine activity in mesolimbic pathway. Evidence: dopamine agonists (amphetamines) trigger psychosis; D2 antagonists (haloperidol) reduce positive symptoms.</p><p><b>Genetics</b>: Gottesman (1991) MZ concordance ~48%, DZ ~17%; not 100% — environment matters.</p>"
    },
    {
      "tag": "Stop 3",
      "h2": "Diathesis-stress & treatment",
      "body_html": "<p>Diathesis-stress: a genetic vulnerability is triggered by stress — childhood trauma, urban upbringing, cannabis use.</p><p>Treatment: <b>antipsychotics</b> (clozapine for resistant cases), <b>CBT for psychosis</b> targeting beliefs about voices, family therapy reducing <b>expressed emotion</b> (Brown et al., 1972).</p>"
    }
  ],
